export const RQ_ANSWERS = {
 "RQ1": "Over eight models, the workflow's decompositions have significantly higher domain independence and business context purity than both baselines, and higher modularity than single-shot prompting, at the price of lower database transaction purity (significant against OpenCode). In a size-controlled supporting view all three gains remain positive, although a little under half of the DI gain over single-shot prompting goes with coarser partitions. The modularity gain is partly by construction; the domain-alignment gains are not optimized and are the independent evidence, although the size of the DI gain depends on the model. Reference similarity is not significantly better⟪: the C2C-50 advantage suggested by our first analysis, with one run per cell, does not hold with more runs and models⟫. All LLM arms outperform traditional tools in cyclic independence and in close correspondence to the reference.",
 "RQ2": "The workflow produced a valid decomposition in 159/160 runs of the primary analysis, more often than OpenCode (a paired test added after the plan), and in 94.4% of attempts over all seventeen models, where it also failed less often than single-shot prompting. Every decomposition it produced was valid, because completeness is guaranteed by construction; its failures were answers that could not be parsed. No approach is stable across repeated runs, even at a fixed seed, and the workflow varies the most.",
 "RQ3": "The workflow used 37.2% fewer tokens than single-shot prompting, in every one of the 32 cells and on average in both input and output, with the least cost variation across models. It processed fewer tokens than OpenCode in total, but most of OpenCode's tokens are cache reads, and outside the cache OpenCode used fewer. Its LLM calls take less time than those of single-shot prompting, but its deterministic stages make a whole run longer. Half of its tokens are spent on evidence construction.",
 "RQ4": "The largest effect we measured came from a deterministic decision: reference-free selection that never grades a candidate by its own construction. Removing the self-referential term from selection and refinement raised modularity, domain independence, and close correspondence to reference services with every model, and cyclic independence over cells, mostly by reducing the fragmentation the term caused; at equal service counts, modularity and business context purity remain higher. Refinement by measurable local search, which replaced an ineffective LLM self-critique, raises structure and domain alignment at the expense of transaction purity. Multiple candidates did not yield better designs; their value is as a safeguard. We did not ablate the LLM components, so these results do not rank deterministic against LLM components in general."
}
